<html>
  <head>
    <title>Buraco - Canasta with a latin touch</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Documentation of the Buraco cardgame">
    <meta name="keywords" content="Documentation, Docu, Buraco, game, card, cardgame">

    <meta name="DC.Creator" content="Markus Schwab">
    <meta name="DC.Date" content="2003-06-22">
    <meta name="DC.Rights" content="Copyright (C) 2003 - 2004, distributed under the GNU Free Documentation License">
  </head>

  <body>
    <h1 align="center">Buraco</h1>
    <hr size=2>

    <p>Buraco is a quite popular game in Venezuela. It is similar to Canasta
      or Romme in Europe; though it tends to end fester, thanks to the
      "Buracos" (a set of extra cards, which one picks up, if one of the team
      does not have any cards left).</p>

    <p>In a nutshell it is about putting down a staple of "matching" cards
      (with the help of its partner) and that way getting rid of all of the
      cards. Matching means that either the numbers must be equal
      (independend of the colour) or the colour and the numbers are in a
      sequence (Ace (as one), three, four, ..., ten, jack, queen, king, ace -
      as the two is a joker, it does not belong to the serie).</p>

    <p>To actually put down the cards, the player must have at least three of
      them; or two which are extended with a joker or a 2 (of whatever
      colour).</p>

    <p>Every player starts with 11 cards in his hand. At the beginning of his
      turn he has either to pick up a card from the staple or he can pick up
      the whole dumped pile, if he has a pair matching the uppermost card
      (without joker). As an exception the player starting the game can pick
      up the card on that pile in the first round, even if he has no matching
      pair.</p>

    <p>Afterwards he can play his cards (with drag and drop<a
      href="Buraco.html.en#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a>) - if he wants: A possible
      way of playing is to keep his cards in the hand to maybe pick up the
      pile of the dumped cards. To start a new pile, he needs - as mentioned
      before - a serie of at least 3 matching cards. Furthermore he can add
      "matching" cards (according to the rules above) to yet existing
      piles.</p>

    <p>If the player can't or want play anymore cards, he must dump one (by
      clicking on it) and the next player (clockwise) continues.</p>

    <p>Further rules are:</p>
    <ul>

      <li>Every pile put down kann only contain a single joker or 2 - except
        if it is a pile of only jokers and/or 2's).</li>

      <li>If one pile contains seven cards, it will be closed (and removed
        from the table). Those containimg either a joker or a 2 are called
        <em>dirty</em> and those without <em>clean</em>. The game can only be
        ended, if the team has at least one of those "cerrados".</li>

      <li>If a player has no cards except of jokers and/or 2's, he picks up
        the "buraco" - further 11 cards of a previously reserved pile (which
        exists one for every team). Afterwards he continues normally (e.g. it
        is still his turn, if he did not dump a card before).</li>

      <li>If there is neither a "buraco" nor has the team a "cerrado" a player
        can not dump his last card.</li>

      <li>Joker or 2's inside a pile can be moved (inside the pile), but not
        picked up again. That means: If there is for example a serie of
        &hearts; 5, 6 and joker, one can move the joker down (to the position
        of the &hearts;4) and therefore add a &hearts;3<a
        href="Buraco.html.en#Note2"><sup>2</sup></a>; but one can not put down
        a &hearts;7 and take the joker in ones hand (though one can put down
        the 7 and the joker becomes the 8 or the 4).</li>

      <li>A joker or 2 on top of the dumped cards can never be picked up; even
        if the player has two equal cards.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>At the end of the game (after a player played his last card or put it
      down), the points are counted. Every "cerrado" without joker or 2 counts
      400 Punkte (otherwise 200); except one with aces - those have 500 or 300
      points respectively. A "cerrado" with joker and 2 counts  1.000 points,
      one with only 2's 2.000.  But <b>attention</b>: If one starts suche a
      pile without finishing it, it also has 1.000 points - but negative!</p>

    <p>Furhter 100 points one get for taking up the buraco and for ending the
      game.</p>

    <p>Finally one adds the points of the cards itself: Those on the table are
      added and those in the hand are substracted. A joker counts 50 points, a
      2 25, the ace counts as 20, king to 8 (inclusively) have 10 and the
      others below only 5 points.</p>

    <p>The game ends, if one of the teams has 2.000 points or more.</p>

    <p>The status line gives some information about what's going on!</p>

    <h2>Note to the layout</h2>
    <p>After starting a game you have (from left to right and from top to
      bottom):</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The staple: A heap of cards (with face down), from where you put
        up new cards (if you put the mouse over it you are informed about
        the remaining cards).</li>

      <li>The pile with the dumped cards (with face up). Those pile also shows
        the number of its cards, if the mouse is put over.</li>

      <li>The cards of the player. Presorted by numbers, but re-sortable with
        drag and drop<a href="Buraco.html.en#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a>.</li>

      <li>The piles on the table of team 1. Cards can be added to that piles
        with drag und drop<a href="Buraco.html.en#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a> (if
        it is the turn of the player).</li>

       <li>The piles on the table of team 2.</li>

      <li>In the last row are the cards of the other (computer) player.</li>
     </ul>

    <hr size=1 noshade>
    <p style="text-indent:-0.3cm;margin-left:0.3cm"><a name="Note1"></a>1)
      Drag and drop means clicking with the <em>right</em> (or the middle)
      mouse button on the card, holding the button and then dragging the card
      to its destination. The end of this activity is signaled by releasing
      the button.</p>

    <p style="text-indent:-0.3cm;margin-left:0.3cm"><a name="Note2"></a>2)
      You don't have to move the joker manually. Just drop the card you want
      to play and the joker moves itself automatically.</p>

